From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenCode <opencode@localhost>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 19:20:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] DIAG ONLY: run KVM init late, so the watchdog is armed first

Diagnostic scaffold, compiled in only with KCFLAGS=-DDIAG_KVM_LATE_INIT.

Measured on 2026-10-30 (docs/evidence/2026-10-30-custom-kernel-hang.txt):

  * drivers/watchdog/sprd_wdt_fiq.c registers its driver with
    late_initcall(sprd_wdt_fiq_init_ops), NOT module_platform_driver(), so
    sprd_wdt_fiq_probe() - where diag-only-arm-aon-watchdog.patch arms the
    AON watchdog for 30 s - is a late_initcall.
  * KVM is built in: virt/kvm/arm/arm.c has module_init(arm_init), i.e. a
    device_initcall, and every device_initcall runs before every
    late_initcall.  Link order ($(drivers-y) before $(virt-y)) only breaks
    ties inside one level and cannot reorder across levels.
  * Consequence, observed: boot_diagwdt_avb.img on boot_a hangs on the logo
    and the 30 s arm never fires.  The bootloader log shows exactly one
    slot_a boot attempt and no reset afterwards, i.e. the SoC never reset.

So the arm sits behind KVM init, not in front of it, and a stall in KVM init
is invisible forever.

This hunk moves KVM's initcall to late_initcall.  Both are then late_initcalls
and the same link order puts the watchdog probe first: the timer is armed,
then arm_init()/kvm_init() runs.  A hang inside KVM is now followed by a
genuine AON watchdog reset ~30 s later, which is the first thing this platform
offers that can make the stall observable (console=null, no VT, no pstore
without a reset).

Nothing else changes; KVM is not used by anything at boot.

diff --git a/virt/kvm/arm/arm.c b/virt/kvm/arm/arm.c
--- a/virt/kvm/arm/arm.c
+++ b/virt/kvm/arm/arm.c
@@ -1521,4 +1521,13 @@ static int arm_init(void)
 	return rc;
 }
 
+#ifdef DIAG_KVM_LATE_INIT
+/*
+ * DIAG build only (KCFLAGS=-DDIAG_KVM_LATE_INIT).
+ *
+ * device_initcall -> late_initcall: the AON watchdog probe is itself a
+ * late_initcall (see drivers/watchdog/sprd_wdt_fiq.c), and $(drivers-y)
+ * links before $(virt-y), so within this level the watchdog is armed
+ * before KVM initialises. A stall in kvm_init() then resets the board
+ * instead of stopping it forever.
+ */
+late_initcall(arm_init);
+#else
 module_init(arm_init);
+#endif
